COMMAND NAME: groupSession

Starts an interactive command-line session with multiple hosts specified 
in a host file. Will also perform SSH key exchanges as the current user 
(or the user specified in the host file).


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

groupSession [-p <ssh_port>] [-l <logfile>] [-s | -x] [-v] [-d] [-?] <host_file>


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The groupSession utility starts an interactive command-line session that 
allows you to run bash shell commands on multiple hosts at the same time. 
The groupSession utility uses a host list file to determine the hosts 
(and optionally the users) involved in a session. You cannot change the 
host list file mid-session. If you want to change the host list file, 
you must start a new groupSession instance after editing the file. 

The groupSession utility requires Python 2.3 or higher. Python is installed 
with most operating systems by default. If you have an older version of 
Python, you must upgrade to 2.3 or higher in order to use groupSession.

The groupSession utility will also exchange SSH keys between the hosts 
at the start of a session if you do not have a trusted host environment 
established already. This allows SSH connections to the remote hosts 
without a password prompt. If a user name is not specified in the host 
list file, then the currently logged in user will be used. 

Greenplum recommends performing the key exchange process twice: once as 
root and once as the gpadmin user (the user designated to own your Greenplum 
Database installation). The Greenplum Database management scripts require 
that the same non-root user be created on all hosts in the Greenplum 
Database system, and the scripts must be able to connect as that user to 
all hosts without a password prompt.

Once your trusted host environment is established, you get the groupSession 
command prompt (=>). You can enter a command as you would in a regular bash 
terminal command-line, and the command will be executed on all hosts in the 
host list file. To run a command on the local host only, prefix the command 
with ! or shell. Use rcopy to copy a file to a specified location on all 
hosts.

ABOUT PATHS AND USERS

If a user name is not specified in the host list file, groupSession will 
execute commands as the currently logged in user. To determine the currently 
logged in user, do a whoami command. If changing users, you should force a 
login by using a dash character (-) after the su command. For example:

 su - [user_name]

By default, groupSession goes to $HOME of the current user on the remote 
hosts after login. To ensure commands are executed correctly on all remote 
hosts, you should always enter absolute paths.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

<host_file>

 Required. Must be listed as the last argument in the command line. 
 The name of a file that contains a list of hosts that will participate 
 in the group session. The groupSession utility will also exchange SSH 
 keys as either the current user or the username specified in the host file. 
 The format of the host file is as follows:

  username@host1
  username@host2
  ...
  username@hostN

  OR (if you want to connect as the current user)

  host1
  host2
  ...
  hostN

-p <ssh_port>

  Optional. If you are using a different SSH port besides the default 
  port of 22, use this option to specify the SSH port number.

-l <logfile>

 Creates a log file for groupSession using the given path and file name. 
 Log files are rolled over at 6 MB, and up to 6 log files are kept at a time.

-s (disable key exchange)

 Optional. Disable the setup of trusted host key exchange. The default 
 behavior is to exchange SSH keys as the specified user between the 
 current host and the hosts in the host file.

-x (exchange keys with all hosts)

 Optional. Using this option will exchange SSH keys between the current host 
 and all hosts in the host file and also interchange keys between the hosts 
 listed in the host file.

-v (verbose mode)

 Optional. Verbose mode will report all commands sent to a host and the 
 output received.

-d (debug mode)

 Optional. Debug mode will report all commands sent to a host and the 
 output received plus additional debug messages.

-? (help)

 Displays the online help.


*****************************************************
INTERACTIVE SESSION COMMANDS 
*****************************************************

At the groupSession command prompt (=>), you can enter a command as you 
would in a regular bash terminal command-line and the command will be 
executed on all hosts in the host list file.

To run a command on the local host only, prefix the command with ! or shell. 
Use the rcopy command to copy a file to a specified location on all hosts.

=> <command>

 Enter any command as you would at a regular bash terminal command-line. 
 This will execute the given command on all of the hosts in the host list 
 file. You cannot use groupSession to start an interactive command session 
 - commands must be executed one at a time.

=> shell <command>

 Commands prefixed with shell will run the command on the local host only.

=> ! <command>

 Commands prefixed with ! will run the command on the local host only.

=> rcopy [<filename> [<path_on_remote_hosts>]]

 The rcopy command is used to copy the specified file to the specified 
 directory location on all of the hosts in the host list file.

=> exchangeKeys

 Exchange ssh keys between all hosts involved in the session. 

=> exit | quit | q

 Exit groupSession.

*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Start a groupSession with all hosts listed in the host_file:

 groupSession host_file

Start groupSession and exchange SSH keys as the current user between the 
current host and all hosts listed in host_file:

 groupSession -x host_file

At the groupSession command prompt, copy a file to the specified location 
on all the hosts in host_file.

 => rcopy gp-2.3.tar.gz /

At the groupSession command prompt, run a shell command on all the 
hosts in host_file.

 => ls -a /dbfast1/gpdata

At the groupSession command prompt, run a shell command on the local host only.

 => shell cat /etc/profile

OR

 => ! cat /etc/profile

